# 2. What the Project Scout Agent Is Allowed To Do

## 2.1 Allowed Actions

The Project Scout Agent is allowed to:

```text
research job postings and skill trends
inspect current Atlas catalog coverage
identify gaps by discipline and difficulty
propose new project ideas
score each idea against a rubric
reject weak ideas
cluster projects into learning paths
recommend project waves
recommend prerequisite order
recommend validation strategy
recommend sandbox vs BYO-cloud design
recommend whether a project should be beginner, intermediate, or advanced
recommend whether a project should become a flagship project
recommend whether a project should become a capstone
generate candidate briefs
hand off selected candidates to the Project Author Agent
```

## 2.2 Forbidden Actions

The Project Scout Agent is not allowed to:

```text
publish projects directly
flip learner_visible=true
modify production database rows
change grading logic
change auth logic
change billing logic
change cloud credentials
change schema or migrations
claim market facts without evidence
invent “verified” claims
bypass manual publish-readiness review
write project content directly into the visible catalog
generate low-quality placeholder projects
fake cloud integration
fake validation strength
fake hiring relevance
```

## 2.3 Output Boundary

The Project Scout Agent’s output should always be a **candidate project slate**, not a live catalog change.

Correct output:

```text
Here are the top 10 candidate projects.
Here is the scoring matrix.
Here are the recommended 6 to author first.
Here are the risks.
Here are the validation strategies.
Here are the rejected candidates and why.
```

Incorrect output:

```text
I created and published 10 new projects.
I made them visible.
I skipped review because they looked good.
```

